---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';

const title = "The Consideration Period: What the Single Code Says About Airport Drop-offs | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "The old 5-minute grace period rule no longer applies. Here is what the Single Code of Practice says about consideration periods at airport drop-off zones.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/five-minute-consideration-period/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "The consideration period: what the Single Code says about airport drop-offs",
  "description": description,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-04-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-24"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader title="The consideration period: what the Single Code says about airport drop-offs" breadcrumbLabel="Consideration period" breadcrumbHref="/guides/five-minute-consideration-period/" updatedDate="NOVEMBER 2026" fixedUpdatedDate={true} />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <div class="callout">
          An older version of this article said BPA members must allow a 5-minute grace period. That is no longer the rule. The Single Code of Practice has replaced the old BPA and IPC codes. Its rules applied from 1 October 2024, with some signage rules phased in.
        </div>

        <h2>What a consideration period is</h2>
        <p>A consideration period is the time a driver gets to read the terms and decide whether to accept them. If the driver does not accept, they can leave.</p>
        <p>The Code lists what the operator must take into account. These include the time to read the signs, the time to pay, and the time to leave. The layout and traffic also count (5.1).</p>
        <p>It is not free parking. The Code says so in the commentary to Annex B.</p>

        <h2>What it says about airport drop-offs</h2>
        <p>The Code sets a minimum consideration period for each type of land. For short-stay land, the minimum is 1 minute (Annex B, Table B.1).</p>
        <p>Short stay means land where you may park for 30 minutes or less. The Code gives airport drop-off zones as an example (5.2, Note 2).</p>
        <p>The same note says the grace period does not apply to short-stay areas. A grace period is extra time after the permitted period ends. So there is no 5-minute or 10-minute grace period at a drop-off zone. This appears in a note. The Code says some notes only explain how to read it.</p>

        <h2>When the consideration period ends early</h2>
        <p>The period can end sooner than the minimum. The Code gives examples: the driver parks and leaves, pays the tariff, or stays for more than 5 minutes (5.1, Note 2; Annex B).</p>
        <p>The operator should keep evidence of how the period ended (Annex B commentary).</p>

        <h2>Is it still a ground for appeal?</h2>
        <p>Only in narrow cases. It may help if you were on the land for a very short time and the operator is treating you as parked on time alone. It does not help if you stayed longer than the minimum.</p>
        <p>If you rely on it, ask the operator for the images with their time stamps. The Code says images must carry an accurate time and date stamp (7.3). You can also ask what consideration period the operator registered for the site. Operators must register this with their trade association before issuing charges (Annex B, B.2).</p>
        <p>Some airport roads are treated as no-stopping zones. The Code has separate rules for those (5.3). Check how the operator classes the site.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key point:</strong> The old 5-minute grace period argument is out of date. At an airport drop-off, the Code's minimum is 1 minute, and there is no grace period.
        </div>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice, version 1.1. Clauses 2.9, 5.1 (and Note 2), 5.2 (Note 2), 5.3, 7.3, and Annex B including Table B.1. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://irp.cdn-website.com/262226a6/files/uploaded/sector+single+Code+of+Practice+Version+1.1+130426-2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IPC-hosted PDF</a></li>
        </ol>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
